```lean
theorem warpedPolarMetric_eq_zero_iff (j) (p v) :
    warpedPolarMetric j p v v = 0 ↔ v.1 = 0 ∧ (j p.1 = 0 ∨ v.2 = 0) := by
  unfold warpedPolarMetric
  constructor
  · intro h
    have h1 : v.1 * v.1 = 0 := by
      nlinarith [mul_self_nonneg v.1, mul_nonneg (sq_nonneg (j p.1)) (inner_self_nonneg), h]
    -- from v.1 * v.1 = 0: v.1 = 0: sq_eq_zero_iff? `mul_self_eq_zero` ✓
    have hv1 : v.1 = 0 := mul_self_eq_zero.mp h1
    refine ⟨hv1, ?_⟩
    -- remaining: (j p.1)^2 * inner v.2 v.2 = 0 → j = 0 ∨ v.2 = 0
    have h2 : (j p.1) ^ 2 * inner ℝ v.2 v.2 = 0 := by nlinarith [h, hv1]
    -- (j²)(⟪v,v⟫) = 0 and both ≥ 0 → one is 0
    rcases mul_eq_zero.mp h2 with hsq | hinn
    · left; exact sq_eq_zero_iff.mp hsq
    · right; exact (inner_self_eq_zero.mp? hinn) -- inner_self_eq_zero : ⟪x,x⟫_ℝ = 0 ↔ x = 0 ✓
  · rintro ⟨hv1, h⟩
    rcases h with hj | hv2
    · rw [hv1, hj]; norm_num  -- 0*0 + 0² * inner = 0: ring
    · rw [hv1, hv2]; simp [inner_zero_right]  -- hmm inner v.2 v.2 with v.2 = 0 → inner 0 0 = 0: simp ✓
```

`inner_self_eq_zero` is an iff: `⟪x, x⟫_𝕜 = 0 ↔ x = 0` ✓ (in RealInnerProductSpace). `sq_eq_zero_iff` : x^2 = 0 ↔ x = 0 ✓ hmm name might be `sq_eq_zero_iff` ✓ exists.

```lean
theorem warpedPolarMetric_pos_def (j) {p v} (hv : v ≠ 0) (hj : j p.1 ≠ 0) :
    0 < warpedPolarMetric j p v v := by
  unfold warpedPolarMetric
  have hsq : 0 < (j p.1)^2 := sq_pos_of_ne_zero hj
  -- cases: v.1 ≠ 0 → v.1² > 0 → sum > 0; v.1 = 0 → v.2 ≠ 0 → inner > 0 → j²·inner > 0
  by_cases hv1 : v.1 = 0
  · have hv2 : v.2 ≠ 0 := by intro h; apply hv; ext <;> simp [hv1, h]
    have hinn : 0 < inner ℝ v.2 v.2 := inner_self_pos.mpr hv2  -- hmm inner_self_pos: x ≠ 0 → 0 < ⟪x,x⟫ ✓
    have hpos : 0 < (j p.1)^2 * inner ℝ v.2 v.2 := mul_pos hsq hinn
    nlinarith [mul_self_nonneg v.1, hpos]
  · have hv1sq : 0 < v.1 * v.1 := mul_self_pos.mpr hv1  -- mul_self_pos : a ≠ 0 → 0 < a*a ✓
    nlinarith [hv1sq, mul_nonneg (sq_nonneg (j p.1)) (inner_self_nonneg)]
```

Careful: `hv : v ≠ 0` with v : ℝ × E: v = 0 ⟺ v.1 = 0 ∧ v.2 = 0. `ext` on Prod: v ≠ 0 → ¬(v.1 = 0 ∧ v.2 = 0). ext with Prod.ext: `apply hv; ext <;> simp [hv1, h]` — hv : (v.1, v.2) ≠ 0; to get contradiction: suffices v = 0: `apply hv; ext <;> simp [hv1, h]` — ext gives goals v.1 = 0 ✓ (hv1) and v.2 = 0 ✓ (h). Good.

